WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.375
I'm really happy
that we're going

00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:06.210
to be able to have time to talk
with Film Editor Andrew Marcus.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.660
Andrew's career spans
over three decades.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.960
His films include Howard's
End, American Psycho,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.530
Under the Tuscan Sun,
Dirty Dancing, Sing Street,

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.790
Isn't Tt Romantic
and, of course,

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:21.540
the film we're celebrating
today - Hedwig and the Angry

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.300
Inch for its 20th anniversary.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.450
So, Andrew, let's
talk about Hedwig.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:28.930
I'm super excited.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:32.800
I love Hedwig and I love every
opportunity to talk about it.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.110
Fantastic!

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.140
So I think the first thing
we'd like to know is,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:42.480
and I think kind of like
actors, there's probably

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.090
somewhat of a casting
process for editors

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.900
where people are looking for a
certain aesthetic or whatever.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.990
So tell us how you came
on board and how you came

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.710
to be chosen for this project.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:57.560
Well, I have a very
circuitous route to Hedwig.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.020
I was doing period
movies in London.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.140
All the Merchant Ivory and
Kenneth Branagh movies.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.920
And I really wanted to
break out of that world

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.160
and do more edgy projects.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.170
So I started working
with a company called

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:16.860
Muse Productions in
Los Angeles, and they

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:23.130
did Trees Lounge, Two Girls
and a Guy, Virgin Suicides.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:29.710
And we were looking for
projects to produce.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.830
And I had seen
Hedwig at SqueezeBox,

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:34.840
and then I had seen
it at Westbeth,

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.760
and when it opened
at Jane Street,

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.270
I brought all the
producers at Muse

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:43.990
and some of their investors
to a number of productions.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.440
I was really excited about
trying to bring it to film.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.185
Unfortunately, Jersey
Productions, Danny Devito's

00:01:52.185 --> 00:01:55.470
company was also
involved, and we got out

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.660
of the running really quickly.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.830
But then when I was
editing American Psycho,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.290
we were editing in
the apartment complex

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.800
that Christine Vachon
lived in, and my husband

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.880
was working for Marlene,
Christine's wife,

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.100
and she just casually
mentioned that she

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.160
was going to produce Hedwig,
and I literally stalked her

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.760
every day to get the job.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:21.870
And I did.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:22.800
Eventually.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.110
That's a great story,
and I love again

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.870
the idea of you going
from Merchant Ivory

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.480
into Hedwig and American Psycho.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.910
So you definitely got
to make your transition.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:33.900
Congratulations.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.750
That's not always easy to do.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.460
What were some of the
biggest challenges

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.400
that you faced with Hedwig?

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.970
And, you know, it's such
a wide variety of styles,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.990
and types of things going on.

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:53.280
There's the stage shows,
there's the animation.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.700
I mean, it's a really
visual, diverse film.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.380
And how did you make the
editing style all fit together,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.710
and what were your
challenges as you

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.380
began combining all of this?

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.650
I mean, to John's credit,
he had workshopped it

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:15.450
at Sundance for a
year before we began.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:19.560
And he storyboarded it and
he's an incredible student

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.150
of cinema.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:27.090
And so, a lot of the challenges
of adapting it to film he had

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.295
worked out before I came on.

00:03:29.295 --> 00:03:31.170
There weren't a lot of
challenges filmically.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.200
The real challenges
were on stage

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.280
John was Tommy
Gnosis and Hedwig.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.360
And then when we had another
actor playing Tommy Gnosis.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.200
That in the end it's very easy
if you're the same character

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.360
to merge into the same person.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.580
But I think that our biggest
challenge was creating

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:56.280
an ending that was moving
where they became one,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.880
as in the Origin of
Love story, but they

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.310
were two separate actors.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.360
And that took a long
time to work out,

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.040
and it took a lot of sort
of reediting and rethinking

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:09.810
how the ending was working.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.650
What about the
animation part of it?

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:15.120
What was the challenge in that,
and how was that accomplished?

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.079
I would say that
wasn't a challenge.

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:20.490
Working with Emily Hubley was
one of the greatest experiences

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:21.300
of my career.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:26.190
She's a complete genius,
and because the animations

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.980
were roughly projected
while we were shooting it,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.700
we had a lot of the
animations as stills

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.140
before we even began shooting.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:40.230
So we had it roughed
out, and it was sort

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.820
of an embarrassment of riches.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.950
The real challenge was
how do you cut away

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.160
from a great performance
to animation?

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.800
And where do you want
to be at any one time

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.980
because to tell the
story of Origin of Love

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.310
you really need the
animations, but you also

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.900
want to hear John singing
it, and the band playing it.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.850
So that was the real challenge.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.500
Finding that balance.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.440
We noticed that you also
have a credit for Second Unit

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.440
Director, and I'm not
sure that that's always

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.610
the case with Film Editors.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.030
How did that come about?

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.630
I was a Second Unit Director
in a lot of the Merchant Ivory

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:21.150
movies, and on Kenneth
Branagh's movies, and on Psycho.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.330
So it's really easy for an
editor to do second unit

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.520
because you've cut
the sequence, and you

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.270
know exactly what pieces
you need to piece in.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.580
It makes sense for
editors to do it.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:37.800
I'm surprised more editors don't
do it, but I love doing it.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:42.830
Things like the gummy
bears, all the insert shots

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:48.360
like the candy going to Luther,
the gummy bears, the bathtub

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.320
scene, and that sort of thing.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.440
So you worked with Hedwig
and the gummy bears.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.910
That is true dexterity.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:56.475
I'm very impressed with that.

00:05:56.475 --> 00:05:58.900
It was a challenge getting
the gummy bears to steam up.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.620
[LAUGHING]

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:02.670
That's fantastic.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:03.510
Thank you.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:08.520
So I assume that you were
brought onto the film

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.110
even as it was in production.

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.210
Where you on the
set for the shoot?

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:16.230
I was actually cutting in a
Winnebago on set at all times,

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:18.630
and it was such a
great experience

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.630
because I was
running to the camera

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.250
when they would ask questions.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:24.310
"Do we need this shot?

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:25.380
Do we need that shot?"

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.060
People could come
to the Winnebago

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.550
and see a sequence
as we were going.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:33.630
And because of that, there was
a real sense of camaraderie

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:37.620
that you don't get any
more really on film sets.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.660
We all were staying
in the same place.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.120
We all spent weekends together.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:48.330
There's a whole sequence when
Hedwig teaches Tommy, goes over

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:52.710
his record collection, and
that was shot on a Saturday

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.350
in the house I was staying in.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:58.170
We just did some
guerilla filmmaking.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:06:59.760
Fantastic.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.060
I get that sense of
camaraderie, and I

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.030
know that that's something
that you don't experience

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:09.480
on every film, and the ones
you do, I think people really

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.250
cherish even all the more just
because of the fun you have

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.930
and the family
that you build so--

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.690
We were all great
friends for a long time

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:21.300
and still can see
each other and talk.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.440
That's great.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:27.600
So as an editor you end
up watching the film

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:28.630
multiple times.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:30.420
So what are some of
the favorite moments

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.790
that you still get a big
kick out of as you're

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:34.140
thinking about Hedwig.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:37.890
I think the most successful
transformation from stage

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.260
to film is Wig in a Box.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.950
It's sort of a perfect
film moment where--

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:52.200
When I did Isn't It Romantic, we
talked about favorite makeover

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.440
scenes, and there are
lists all over the internet

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.370
about favorite makeover scenes.

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.750
And I think Wig in
a Box and Clueless

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:03.900
come up as the two
favorite makeover scenes.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:10.050
I think Ari and costumes, the
production design, the camera,

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:12.510
everything sort of came
together in that scene

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:16.530
and made it really special,
and really cinematic.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:18.990
And really kind of
thrilling for the audience.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.520
So you mentioned
production design.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:24.510
You have any memories of
working with Therese DePrez

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:28.320
or any reflections on her
work seeing it as an editor

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.300
and how it worked for you?

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.340
I loved working with Therese.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.970
She taught me so much
about production design,

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:38.510
and the value of
production design.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:44.059
When we did Bilgewaters, which
is where Hedwig was booked

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:47.390
to perform, it was a
decommissioned Red Lobster

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:51.050
outside of Toronto,
and she redecorated

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:53.845
it to show that it was a
different place every time.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:59.630
One day, I picked up
the menus, and she

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:03.380
had written these extensive
menus that were hysterical.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:07.300
Everything was a
joke on the menu

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.740
and you never saw
the menu on camera,

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:13.160
but that was just
her level of detail.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:15.530
The motto of the restaurant
was 'Bilgewaters -

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:18.410
yesterday's seafood
at tomorrow's prices.'

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:21.440
And you never saw that, but
there is just sort of a gift

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:23.690
to everybody on set
that she gave us.

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:28.040
I love the idea of
that being in your head

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:32.570
as you're performing on
the set, and sitting there

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:33.710
in that location.

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:35.110
A decommissioned Red Lobster.

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:39.650
I Love all the things that would
put into your head as an actor.

00:09:39.650 --> 00:09:42.590
And it's great for extras,
it's great for everybody.

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.050
It just adds a
whole other layer.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:46.220
Fantastic.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:48.920
So we're in a very unusual time.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:50.240
Very particular time.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:53.810
And at the same time,
this film at 20 years

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:59.030
still has a resonance, and still
has I think messages for us

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:01.070
to be reminded of regularly.

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:06.200
So tell me, how do you
feel this film still

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:08.030
impacts 20 years later?

00:10:08.030 --> 00:10:11.210
I think the film was
way ahead of itself.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:15.810
And people didn't
quite understand

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:19.200
what John was getting at
when he was making it.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.740
I think John was
always really clear

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.490
that Hedwig isn't a
drag queen, Hedwig

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:29.730
is a genderqueer performer.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:33.420
And the whole notion of
genderqueer, and gender

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:36.960
fluidity, it's now
taken root and people

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:38.340
accept it completely.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:43.440
But I remember bringing really
like sophisticated people

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.040
to the show.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:47.760
I brought someone who is
like a part of Andy Warhol's

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.300
Factory as a possible investor
and they didn't get it at all.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:56.130
And I think it was just way,
way, way ahead of its time.

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:57.240
What it was saying.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:59.640
What it was trying to achieve.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:04.050
I also think one of the
sad things when it came out

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:08.020
was gay rock 'n roll
wasn't a big thing.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:11.940
And so I remember somebody
saying it was too rock

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:16.020
'n roll for the gays, and
too gay for the rockers.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:19.290
And I think now it's found
its place completely,

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:20.640
and it's not shocking at all.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:24.060
I just showed it to
my 13-year-old twins

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:26.490
and they think it's the
greatest thing I've worked on.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:28.945
Finally, something cool
that I've worked on.

00:11:28.945 --> 00:11:29.850
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:31.320
Wonderful.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:34.150
They'll grow to love
Howard's End as time passes.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:35.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.890
Anyway, I do want to ask you
this since you brought it

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:45.932
up, and in talking about it
just now, what as an editor,

00:11:45.932 --> 00:11:47.640
what was working with
the music, and what

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.670
were your impressions
of the music aspects

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:55.140
of Hedwig, and how that fit in,
and the contribution that that

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:56.100
made to the film?

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.290
I mean, I love
working with music.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.110
I'm a secret music
theater queen,

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:07.260
and I just think songs convey
emotion way better than words.

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:11.550
And whether it's like Dirty
Dancing, which I worked on,

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:15.330
or Sing Street, or
I think anything

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:17.530
is enhanced with music.

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:19.950
And so it was a real privilege
to work on this because I

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:21.920
think the songs are so amazing.

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.700
But, it also gives
you a shortcut.

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:28.130
You don't have to do
a lot of exposition.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:30.290
You don't have to do--

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:34.370
When you were asking me about
what favorite scenes I had,

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:37.370
I think Wig in a Box
is a favorite because

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:39.800
of the makeover aspect.

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:45.290
But even re-watching it for
this, when Hedwig puts on--

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:49.730
gives the wig to Miriam
in Midnight Radio,

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:54.050
and then she falls into people,
I tear up at that still.

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:57.620
And I think you couldn't
get that level of emotion

00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:59.240
in just a dialogue scene.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.420
It's completely conveyed by
the combination of music,

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:05.890
and acting, and
performance, and it takes it

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:07.670
to a whole other level.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:11.110
Well, it's really been fun
having a look at Hedwig

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:12.940
from the insights
of the Film Editor.

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:16.000
I'm really always happy to have
as many of the collaborators,

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:19.390
and many of the perspectives
talking about a film.

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:21.220
And I really appreciate
your taking time

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:23.990
with us for this
conversation today.

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:25.420
Thanks so much, Andrew.

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:26.903
Thank you.

